  "evidence": "TEXT_E: Rebuild the NHS (end internal market, treat 100,000 extra patients, real-terms spending rises), ban tobacco advertising, independent Food Standards Agency; cut infant class sizes and universal nursery for 4‑year‑olds; welfare‑to‑work for 250,000 under‑25s, national childcare, retain/uprate Child Benefit; National Minimum Wage via Low Pay Commission and union recognition by worker vote; fiscal ‘golden rule’, Bank of England reform, no rise in basic/top income tax, clamp down on fraud, sentencing guidelines and support for victims. TEXT_C: Social rights in a written constitution; NHS investment and prevention focus, ban tobacco advertising; increases to child benefit and pensions, end means‑testing for residential care, free nursery for 3–4‑year‑olds, restore student grants; minimum wage at half median male earnings and strong trade union rights; business tax cuts and remove NI ceiling for higher earners; independence from the UK, reform the monarchy, abolish House of Lords role, leave NATO and remove Trident.",
  "reasoning": "Both texts score highly on Care and Equality, but TEXT_E couples these with clear proportionality cues (golden rule, outcome measurement, Low Pay Commission setting the minimum wage, anti‑fraud, victim support) that map to the profile’s nontrivial Proportionality weight. TEXT_C adds strong authority‑subverting and loyalty‑disrupting planks (UK break‑up, leave NATO, reduce monarchy/House of Lords roles) that conflict with even the electorate’s modest Authority/Loyalty weights, making TEXT_E the closer fit overall.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_E"
}
